{"content":[{"signature":"","thinking":"\nLet me analyze this task carefully.\n\nThe task SE-PE-01 requires implementing a `solve(case)` function that takes a payload with:\n- `nodes`: a list of nodes, each with `id`, `parent`, `symbol`, `depth`, `removable`, `utility`\n- `required_symbols`: a list of symbols that must be present\n\nThe output should be:\n- `removed_node_ids`: list of node IDs that are removed\n- `retained_node_ids`: list of node IDs that are retained\n\nLet me understand the logic:\n\nLooking at the fixtures:\n- There's a tree structure (nodes with parent references)\n- Some nodes are `removable: true`, some are `removable: false`\n- There are `required_symbols` that must be present in the retained tree\n- Each node has a `utility` value\n\nThe task seems to be about pruning a tree while:\n1. Keeping nodes whose symbols are in `required_symbols` (or ensuring those symbols are covered)\n2. Removing nodes that can be removed\n\nLet me think more carefully. The key constraints seem to be:\n1. Non-removable nodes cannot be removed\n2. If a node is removed, all its descendants should also be removed (subtree removal)\n3. The `required_symbols` constraint means we need to retain at least one node with each required symbol\n\nWait, let me re-read. The task doesn't explicitly state the pruning criteria beyond what can be inferred from the fixtures. Let me look at the fixture cases more carefully.\n\nCase 000:\n- Nodes: root(root, not removable), n1(s1, not removable), n2(s2, removable), n3(s0, not removable), n4(s1, removable, parent=n2), n5(s2, not removable, parent=n3), n6(s0, removable, parent=n1)\n- required_symbols: [\"root\", \"s1\"]\n\nSince n4 has symbol \"s1\" which is required, but n4 is under n2 which is removable...\n- n1 has symbol \"s1\" and is not removable - so \"s1\" is satisfied by n1\n- \"root\" is satisfied by the root node\n\nSo n2 (s2, removable) could potentially be removed. If n2 is removed, n4 (s1, removable) is also removed.\nn6 (s0, removable) could be removed.\n\nThe question is: what's the optimization criterion?\n\nLooking at this more carefully - this seems to be a tree pruning problem where:\n1. We cannot remove non-removable nodes\n2. We need to ensure all required_symbols are still covered by retained nodes\n3. We want to maximize (or minimize?) something based on utility\n\nActually, thinking about this differently - maybe the task is simpler: remove all removable nodes unless doing so would violate the required_symbols constraint (i.e., if removing a node would cause a required symbol to have no representative).\n\nLet me check case 000:\n- required_symbols: [\"root\", \"s1\"]\n- Nodes with symbol \"root\": root (not removable) ✓\n- Nodes with symbol \"s1\": n1 (not removable), n4 (removable, but parent n2 is removable)\n\nSince n1 is not removable and has symbol \"s1\", the \"s1\" requirement is satisfied even without n4.\nSo we could remove n2, n4, n6 (all removable nodes) without violating required_symbols.\n\nBut wait - if a parent is removed, its children must be removed too (since the tree structure is broken). So if n2 is removed, n4 must also be removed.\n\nLet me think about what the algorithm should be:\n\nOption 1: Remove all removable nodes (as long as parent isn't removed, child can't survive). But we need to preserve required symbols.\n\nActually, maybe the algorithm is:\n1. Start by trying to remove all removable nodes\n2. But if removing a node would cause a required symbol to be completely absent from the retained tree, keep it\n\nBut there's a subtlety: if we remove a node, we also remove its subtree.\n\nLet me think about this more carefully with a different approach:\n\nThe problem might be: find the minimum set of nodes to retain such that:\n1. All non-removable nodes are retained\n2. All required symbols are covered\n3. Tree structure is maintained (if a node is retained, its parent must be retained)\n4. We want to maximize removal (or minimize retained nodes, or something based on utility)\n\nActually wait - let me reconsider. The problem says \"utility\" for each node. Maybe we want to maximize total utility of retained nodes? Or maybe the task is purely structural.\n\nLooking at the required_symbols: [\"root\", \"s1\"] for all cases. Nodes that have these symbols:\n- \"root\": only the root node\n- \"s1\": n1 (depth 1, not removable), n4 (depth 2, removable, parent n2 is removable)\n\nFor case 002:\n- n4 has utility 0.80397101 (highest among removable nodes)\n- n6 has utility 0.57371477\n- n2 has utility 0.97660242\n\nIf we're trying to maximize utility, we'd want to keep high-utility nodes. But n2 is removable and doesn't have a required symbol.\n\nHmm, I'm overcomplicating this. Let me re-read the problem statement more carefully.\n\nThe task says \"SE-PE-01\" which could stand for \"Symbol-Exclusion Pruning Experiment 01\" or something similar. The description says nothing about maximizing utility - it just asks which nodes to remove and which to retain.\n\nActually, I think I need to figure out the algorithm from the fixture data alone. But the fixtures only show the INPUT shapes, not the expected outputs. So I need to infer the algorithm.\n\nLet me reconsider the problem. The key fields are:\n- `removable`: whether a node can be removed\n- `required_symbols`: symbols that must be present in the retained tree\n- `utility`: possibly used as a tiebreaker or optimization criterion\n\nThe most natural interpretation seems to be:\n1. A node can only be removed if it's marked `removable`\n2. If a node is removed, all its descendants are also removed\n3. The required_symbols must each have at least one node with that symbol in the retained tree\n4. We want to maximize the number of removed nodes (pruning), subject to constraints 1-3\n\nBut there might be a utility-based optimization. Let me think...\n\nActually, with the constraint that removing a parent removes its children too, the structure is:\n- We can only remove a subtree rooted at a removable node\n- But we must keep nodes if their symbol is in required_symbols AND no other retained node has that symbol\n\nWait, but non-removable nodes can never be removed, so if a required symbol has any non-removable node, that symbol is always covered. The only concern is for required symbols where all nodes with that symbol are removable.\n\nLooking at the examples:\n- required_symbols: [\"root\", \"s1\"]\n- \"root\": root node (not removable) - always covered\n- \"s1\": n1 (depth 1, not removable) - always covered\n\nSo in all three cases, all required symbols are covered by non-removable nodes. This means we can safely remove ALL removable nodes!\n\nFor case 000: remove n2, n4, n6 (all removable)\nFor case 001: remove n2, n4, n6 (all removable)\nFor case 002: remove n2, n4, n6 (all removable)\n\nBut wait, if n2 is removed, n4 would also need to be removed (it's a child of n2). But n4 is also removable, so that's fine.\n\nHmm, but what if n4 was NOT removable? Then we'd have a conflict: we can't remove n2 (because it would make n4 orphaned) but we also can't force n4 to stay if its parent is removed...\n\nActually, I think the constraint is:\n- If a node's parent is removed, the node is also removed (regardless of removable flag)\n- But we should never remove a non-removable node (so we shouldn't remove a parent if it would force removal of a non-removable child)\n\nWait, that's the key insight! If a non-removable node exists in the subtree of a removable node, we can't remove that removable node (because that would remove the non-removable child).\n\nLet me re-examine:\n- A removable node can be removed ONLY if:\n  1. It's marked as removable\n  2. None of its descendants are non-removable (otherwise removing it would force removal of non-removable nodes)\n  3. Removing it doesn't cause a required symbol to have zero coverage\n\nActually maybe not. Maybe the tree structure just says: if a node is removed, its subtree is removed. And the constraint is just:\n1. Non-removable nodes cannot be removed\n2. Required symbols must each have at least one retained node\n\nAnd the goal is to remove as many nodes as possible (greedy).\n\nBut we need to be careful: we can only initiate removal from nodes whose parents are retained (or from root-level removable nodes). Actually, removal cascades down: remove a node, and all descendants are removed too.\n\nSo the algorithm might be:\n1. For each removable node (considering from root to leaves), check if removing it (and its subtree) would violate any constraints\n2. If not, remove it and its subtree\n\nLet me think about what \"violating constraints\" means:\n- A required symbol becomes uncovered (zero retained nodes with that symbol)\n- Actually, \"removing a node\" implicitly means non-removable nodes in its subtree... but we said non-removable nodes can't be removed\n\nSo there are two interpretations:\nA) You can only remove a removable node if ALL nodes in its subtree are also removable\nB) Removing a removable node removes the entire subtree, even if some descendants are non-removable (but this would conflict with the non-removable constraint)\n\nI think interpretation A is more consistent: you can only remove a removable node if it has no non-removable descendants. Otherwise, you'd be forcibly removing non-removable nodes.\n\nWith this interpretation for the fixture cases:\n- n2 has child n4 (removable) - no non-removable descendants, so n2 can be removed\n- n6 has no children, and is removable - can be removed\n- n4 has no children, and is removable - can be removed (but if n2 is removed, n4 is also removed)\n\nNow, with required_symbols [\"root\", \"s1\"]:\n- \"root\" is covered by root (non-removable)\n- \"s1\" is covered by n1 (non-removable)\n\nSo removing n2 (and n4), and n6 doesn't violate any required_symbols constraint.\n\nResult for all cases: \n- removed: [n2, n4, n6]\n- retained: [root, n1, n3, n5]\n\nLet me code this up:\n\n```python\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = case[\"nodes\"]\n    required_symbols = set(case[\"required_symbols\"])\n    \n    # Build lookup structures\n    node_by_id = {n[\"id\"]: n for n in nodes}\n    children = {n[\"id\"]: [] for n in nodes}\n    for n in nodes:\n        if n[\"parent\"] is not None:\n            children[n[\"parent\"]].append(n[\"id\"])\n    \n    # Get all descendants of a node (including itself)\n    def get_subtree(node_id):\n        result = [node_id]\n        for child_id in children[node_id]:\n            result.extend(get_subtree(child_id))\n        return result\n    \n    # Check if a subtree has any non-removable nodes\n    def has_non_removable(node_id):\n        if not node_by_id[node_id][\"removable\"]:\n            return True\n        return any(has_non_removable(child_id) for child_id in children[node_id])\n    \n    # Get symbols covered by a set of node IDs\n    def covered_symbols(node_ids):\n        return {node_by_id[nid][\"symbol\"] for nid in node_ids}\n    \n    # Start with all nodes retained\n    retained = set(n[\"id\"] for n in nodes)\n    \n    # Try to remove removable subtrees\n    # Process from leaves upward, or just check each removable node\n    # A node can be removed if:\n    # 1. It's removable\n    # 2. Its parent is still retained (or no parent)\n    # 3. None of its subtree nodes are non-removable (shouldn't forcibly remove non-removable)\n    # 4. Removing its subtree doesn't eliminate coverage for any required symbol\n    \n    # Actually, let me think about order of processing...\n    # We should process removable nodes in some order\n    \n    # Find candidates for removal: removable nodes where entire subtree is removable\n    candidates = []\n    for n in nodes:\n        if n[\"removable\"] and not has_non_removable_child(n[\"id\"]):\n            candidates.append(n[\"id\"])\n    \n    # Hmm, this is getting complex. Let me simplify.\n    # \n    # A removable node can be a \"removal root\" if:\n    # - It's removable\n    # - All nodes in its subtree are also removable (since non-removable can't be removed)\n    # - Its parent is retained\n    \n    # Actually, I think the problem might be simpler: \n    # we just remove all removable nodes that:\n    # 1. Have no non-removable descendants  \n    # 2. Removing them doesn't uncovering a required symbol\n    # And we process from top to bottom (removing a parent removes its children automatically)\n    \n    pass\n```\n\nWait, I'm overcomplicating this. Let me think about it differently.\n\nThe key insight is: if a node is removable, we WANT to remove it (and its subtree), UNLESS:\n1. It has non-removable descendants (we can't forcibly remove those)\n2. Removing it would uncovered a required symbol\n\nLet me re-examine whether non-removable descendants prevent removal:\n\nLooking at case 000 again:\n- n2 (removable) has child n4 (removable) - no non-removable descendants\n- n3 (NOT removable) has child n5 (NOT removable) - n3 can't be removed anyway\n- n1 (NOT removable) has child n6 (removable) - n1 can't be removed anyway\n\nWhat if n5 was under n2? Then n2 would have a non-removable descendant, and we couldn't remove n2.\n\nI think my interpretation is correct:\n- A removable node can be removed only if its entire subtree is also removable\n- Additionally, the required_symbols must still be covered after removal\n\nSo the algorithm:\n1. Find all \"removable subtrees\" - removable nodes where the entire subtree is removable\n2. For each such subtree (from roots down), determine if removing it violates required_symbols\n3. Remove if safe\n\nBut we also need to handle the case where removing a parent removes a child that would have provided a required symbol.\n\nLet me write cleaner code:\n\n```python\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = case[\"nodes\"]\n    required_symbols = set(case[\"required_symbols\"])\n    \n    node_by_id = {n[\"id\"]: n for n in nodes}\n    children = {}\n    for n in nodes:\n        children[n[\"id\"]] = []\n    for n in nodes:\n        if n[\"parent\"] is not None:\n            children[n[\"parent\"]].append(n[\"id\"])\n    \n    def get_subtree_ids(node_id):\n        result = [node_id]\n        for child_id in children[node_id]:\n            result.extend(get_subtree_ids(child_id))\n        return result\n    \n    def subtree_all_removable(node_id):\n        if not node_by_id[node_id][\"removable\"]:\n            return False\n        return all(subtree_all_removable(c) for c in children[node_id])\n    \n    # A node can potentially be \"removal root\" if it's removable and all its descendants are removable\n    # We should process from top to bottom (BFS order by depth)\n    \n    removal_roots = set()\n    retained = set(n[\"id\"] for n in nodes)\n    \n    # Sort by depth to process parents before children\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: n[\"depth\"])\n    \n    for n in sorted_nodes:\n        nid = n[\"id\"]\n        # Skip if already scheduled for removal (parent was removed)\n        if nid not in retained:\n            continue\n        # Check if this node can be a removal root\n        if not n[\"removable\"]:\n            continue\n        # Check if entire subtree is removable\n        if not subtree_all_removable(nid):\n            continue\n        # Get subtree\n        subtree = get_subtree_ids(nid)\n        # Check if removing this subtree would violate required_symbols\n        hypothetical_retained = retained - set(subtree)\n        retained_symbols = {node_by_id[rid][\"symbol\"] for rid in hypothetical_retained}\n        if required_symbols.issubset(retained_symbols):\n            # Safe to remove\n            retained -= set(subtree)\n    \n    removed = [n[\"id\"] for n in nodes if n[\"id\"] not in retained]\n    retained_list = [n[\"id\"] for n in nodes if n[\"id\"] in retained]\n    \n    return {\n        \"removed_node_ids\": removed,\n        \"retained_node_ids\": retained_list\n    }\n```\n\nThis looks reasonable. Let me trace through case 000:\n\nNodes sorted by depth:\n- root (depth 0, not removable) - skip\n- n1 (depth 1, not removable) - skip\n- n2 (depth 1, removable) - check subtree: n2 (removable), n4 (removable) - all removable ✓\n  - Subtree: [n2, n4]\n  - hypothetical retained: {root, n1, n3, n5, n6}\n  - retained symbols: {root, s1, s0, s2}\n  - required_symbols {root, s1} ⊆ {root, s1, s0, s2} ✓\n  - Remove n2, n4\n- n3 (depth 1, not removable) - skip\n- n4 (depth 2, removable) - but n4 is already removed, skip\n- n5 (depth 2, not removable) - skip\n- n6 (depth 2, removable) - check subtree: n6 (removable, no children) - all removable ✓\n  - Subtree: [n6]\n  - hypothetical retained: {root, n1, n3, n5} (n2, n4 already removed)\n  - retained symbols: {root, s1, s0, s2}\n  - required_symbols {root, s1} ⊆ {root, s1, s0, s2} ✓\n  - Remove n6\n\nResult:\n- removed: [n2, n4, n6]\n- retained: [root, n1, n3, n5]\n\nThis seems correct! The same would apply to cases 001 and 002 since the structure is identical.\n\nNow let me also think about edge cases:\n- What if removing a subtree would uncover a required symbol? Then we keep it.\n- What if a removable node has non-removable descendants? We skip it (can't remove it).\n\nThe code looks good. Let me clean it up and also handle the case where we might need to process in order (though since we skip already-removed nodes, it should be fine):\n\nActually, wait. There's a subtle issue: when I check `subtree_all_removable`, I'm checking the entire subtree. But what if a parent node in the subtree is non-removable? The `subtree_all_removable` function checks `node_by_id[node_id][\"removable\"]` first, so if the root of the candidate subtree is not removable, we skip it. And we've already filtered by `n[\"removable\"]` before calling `subtree_all_removable`. But `subtree_all_removable` also checks children recursively, so it handles non-removable descendants correctly.\n\nActually, since we already check `n[\"removable\"]` before calling `subtree_all_removable`, and the function itself checks `node_by_id[node_id][\"removable\"]`, there's some redundancy but no bug.\n\nLet me also consider: should we order by utility to make better decisions? The problem might want us to maximize total utility of removed nodes, or the fixture cases might just happen to have the same outcome regardless.\n\nLooking at the fixture cases, all three have the same structure (just different utility values), and the answer seems to be the same: remove n2, n4, n6. The utility values don't seem to affect the outcome.\n\nI think the algorithm is correct as stated: remove all removable subtrees that don't violate required_symbols, processed top-to-bottom.\n\nLet me finalize the code:\n\n```python\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = case[\"nodes\"]\n    required_symbols = set(case[\"required_symbols\"])\n    \n    node_by_id = {n[\"id\"]: n for n in nodes}\n    children = {n[\"id\"]: [] for n in nodes}\n    for n in nodes:\n        if n[\"parent\"] is not None:\n            children[n[\"parent\"]].append(n[\"id\"])\n    \n    def get_subtree_ids(node_id):\n        result = [node_id]\n        for child_id in children[node_id]:\n            result.extend(get_subtree_ids(child_id))\n        return result\n    \n    def subtree_all_removable(node_id):\n        if not node_by_id[node_id][\"removable\"]:\n            return False\n        return all(subtree_all_removable(c) for c in children[node_id])\n    \n    retained = set(n[\"id\"] for n in nodes)\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: n[\"depth\"])\n    \n    for n in sorted_nodes:\n        nid = n[\"id\"]\n        if nid not in retained:\n            continue\n        if not n[\"removable\"]:\n            continue\n        if not subtree_all_removable(nid):\n            continue\n        subtree = set(get_subtree_ids(nid))\n        hypothetical_retained = retained - subtree\n        retained_symbols = {node_by_id[rid][\"symbol\"] for rid in hypothetical_retained}\n        if required_symbols.issubset(retained_symbols):\n            retained -= subtree\n    \n    all_ids = [n[\"id\"] for n in nodes]\n    removed = [nid for nid in all_ids if nid not in retained]\n    retained_list = [nid for nid in all_ids if nid in retained]\n    \n    return {\n        \"removed_node_ids\": removed,\n        \"retained_node_ids\": retained_list\n    }\n```\n\nThis looks clean and correct. Let me format it as the required JSON output.\n\nThe response must be exactly one valid JSON object with key \"implementation\" containing the Python source as a string.\n","type":"thinking"},{"text":"{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case[\\\"nodes\\\"]\\n    required_symbols = set(case[\\\"required_symbols\\\"])\\n\\n    node_by_id = {n[\\\"id\\\"]: n for n in nodes}\\n    children = {n[\\\"id\\\"]: [] for n in nodes}\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        if n[\\\"parent\\\"] is not None:\\n            children[n[\\\"parent\\\"]].append(n[\\\"id\\\"])\\n\\n    def get_subtree_ids(node_id):\\n        result = [node_id]\\n        for child_id in children[node_id]:\\n            result.extend(get_subtree_ids(child_id))\\n        return result\\n\\n    def subtree_all_removable(node_id):\\n        if not node_by_id[node_id][\\\"removable\\\"]:\\n            return False\\n        return all(subtree_all_removable(c) for c in children[node_id])\\n\\n    retained = set(n[\\\"id\\\"] for n in nodes)\\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: n[\\\"depth\\\"])\\n\\n    for n in sorted_nodes:\\n        nid = n[\\\"id\\\"]\\n        if nid not in retained:\\n            continue\\n        if not n[\\\"removable\\\"]:\\n            continue\\n        if not subtree_all_removable(nid):\\n            continue\\n        subtree = set(get_subtree_ids(nid))\\n        hypothetical_retained = retained - subtree\\n        retained_symbols = {node_by_id[rid][\\\"symbol\\\"] for rid in hypothetical_retained}\\n        if required_symbols.issubset(retained_symbols):\\n            retained -= subtree\\n\\n    all_ids = [n[\\\"id\\\"] for n in nodes]\\n    removed = [nid for nid in all_ids if nid not in retained]\\n    retained_list = [nid for nid in all_ids if nid in retained]\\n\\n    return {\\n        \\\"removed_node_ids\\\": removed,\\n        \\\"retained_node_ids\\\": retained_list\\n    }\\n\"}","type":"text"}],"id":"msg_01qoi2kdUxLPg7ubWNQZW465","model":"claude-opus-4-7","role":"assistant","stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"type":"message","usage":{"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2222},"cache_creation_input_tokens":2222,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"inference_geo":"not_available","input_tokens":55,"output_tokens":5957,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":5474},"service_tier":"standard"}}